WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.740
A lot of pride in that team, in that era.

2
00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:11.600
The people in Mississippi, when I went to seminary up there in ’88 to ’91,

3
00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:16.220
people up there still remembered my name even though I was never a starter,

4
00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:22.720
never a star, but I . . . They would know my name because I played at LSU.

5
00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:26.240
And the rivalry between LSU and Ole Miss was so great at that time.

6
00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:33.300
It was just a great era to be playing in, because there was a lot of focus on the two schools.

7
00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:39.980
And so it’s almost like I’m as well known in Mississippi as I am in Louisiana because of that particular point in time.

8
00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:47.840
And Coach [Paul] Dietzel and Jim Corbett, the athletic director at the time, were the great promoters that made that possible.

9
00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:53.380
The coaching is one thing, but the notoriety we got from that was something else.

10
00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:59.880
I mean, you know, my picture was on the front of Life magazine, had a Chinese coolie mask on.

11
00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:06.550
But there was a lot of excitement about LSU football at that time, and it had to do with the promotional mind of Dietzel and Corbett.
